72.11.2 模型评估 (model evaluation):
> e.g., housing price prediction (x1=size in feet^2, x2=# of bedrooms, x3=# of floors, x4=age of house) 
model fit training data well, but fail to generalize to new data, 
with more features, it is harder to plot fw,b(x) vs. y: 
use like 70% of data to train model, 30% of data to test model, we say:
training set: (x^[1], y^[1]), (x^[2], y^[2]), ..., (x^[mtrain], y^[mtrain])
test set: (xtest^[1], ytest^[1]), (xtest^[2], ytest^[2]), ..., (xtest^[mtest], ytest^[mtest])
> Train/test procedure for linear regression (with squared error cost) :
minimizecost function: J(w,b)=min[(1/2mtrain)*sum(fw,b(x^[i])-y^[i])^2+(lambda/2mtrain)*sum(wj^2)] 
compute test error: Jtest(w,b)=(1/2mtest)*[sum(fw,b(xtest^[i])-ytest^[i])^2]
compute training error: Jtrain(w,b)=(1/2mtrain)*[sum(fw,b(xtrain^[i])-ytrain^[i])^2]
> train/test procedure for classification problem (o/1):
J(w,b)=(-1/m)*sum[y^[i]*log(fw,b(x^[i])) + (1-y^[i])*log(1-fw,b(x^[i]))] + (lambda/2m)*sum(wj^2) 
test error: Jtest(w,b)=(-1/mtest)*sum[ytest^[i]*log(fw,b(xtest^[i])) + (1-ytest^[i])*log(1-fw,b(xtest^[i]))] 
train error: Jtrain(w,b)=(-1/mtrain)*sum[ytrain^[i]*log(fw,b(xtrain^[i])) + (1-ytrain^[i])*log(1-fw,b(xtrain^[i]))]

there is a more common way to be used for definition of j test and j train in classification problem in terms of test error: 
fraction of the test set and fraction of train set that the alogorithm has misclassified: 
y-hat=1 if fw,b(x^[i])>=0.5, y-hat=0 if fw,b(x^[i])<0.5
and then count up in the test set the fraction of example where y-hat!=y (groud truth label)
Jtest(w,b): fraction of test set that has been misclassified 
Jtrain(w,b): fraction of train set that has been misclassified 

73.11.3 模型选择和训练交叉验证测试集
> once w,b are fit to the training set, the training error is likely lower than actual generalization error, while test error is better estimate of how well the model will generalize to new data than train error 
> model selection (not recommended flawed): e.g. house price prediction
1. fw,b(x)=w1.x1+b #--> w1, b1--> Jtest(w1, b1)
2. fw,b(x)=w1.x1+w2.x^2+b #second-order polynomial-->w2, b2--> Jtest(w2, b2)
3. fw,b(x)=w1.x1+w2.x^2+w3.x^3+b #third-order polynomial-->w3, b3--> Jtest(w3, b3)
.....
10. fw,b(x)=w1.x1+w2.x2^2+....+w10.x^10+b #tenth-order polynomial-->w10, b10--> Jtest(w10, b10)
look at these Jtest, and see which is the lowest, and choose that model, then report test set error Jtest(w,b), but it is little optimistic to estimate generalization error,becauase we fit an extra parameter (order of polynomial) to test set
> model selection (recomended): 
spilt data into training set, cross-validation set, test set: e.g., 60% training, 20% cross-validation, 20% test set 
(x^[1],y^[1]), ....(x^[mtrain], y^[mtrain]) 
(xcv^[1], ycv^[1]),.....(xcv^[mcv], ycv^[mcv])
(xtest^[1], ytest^[1]),.....(xtest^[mtest], ytest^[mtest])
cross-validation set (validation set/development set/dev set): extra data set used to cross-check validity or accuracy of model 
>> traning/validation/test: 
training error: Jtrain(w,b)=(1/2mtrain)*sum(fw,b(x^[i])-y^[i])^2 
cross-validation error: Jcv(w,b)=(1/2mcv)*sum(fw,b(xcv^[i])-ycv^[i])^2 
test error: Jtest(w,b)=(1/2mtest)*sum(fw,b(xtest^[i])-ytest^[i])^2  
use the same 10 models as above to fit the parameters w, b, then compute Jcv(w,b) for each model, and choose the model with the lowest Jcv(w,b), then estimate generalization error by computing Jtest(w,b)
this method also work for choosing other types of models: e.g., choosing a neural network architecture:
fit W, B for each layer, compute Jcv(w,b) for each layer, choose the layer with the lowest Jcv(w,b), then estimate generalization error by computing Jtest(w,b) 
> only use training set, cross validation set to choose model and use test set to estimate generalization performance of model

