fw,b(x)=w1x+w2x^2+b #just right fit: Jtrain low, Jcv low 
> understand degree of polynomial vs. Jtrain and Jcv: 
as degree (d) increase, Jtrain decrease, when d is too high or low, Jcv increase, when in the middle, Jcv decrease 
> summary: 
underfit: high bias, Jtrain high, Jtrain close to Jcv 
overfit: high variance, Jtrain maybe low, Jcv more higher
High bias and high variance: Jtrain high, Jcv far more higher (less common in 1D regression) 

high bias mean it is even not doing well in training set, high variance mean it does much worse in cross-validation set than training set 

